## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, race conditions with thread lifecycle, and memory-ordering guarantees in kernel-space futex design.

## Key Concepts
- Futex fast vs slow path
- Per-address wait queues
- Robust futex list and exit handling
- Race conditions during thread termination
- Memory barriers and wake ordering

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of fast-path vs slow-path for futex_wait
typedef struct { atomic_int *addr; wait_queue_t *wq; robust_list_t *robust; } futex_t;
int futex_wait(int *addr, int expected) {
  // Fast path: if *addr != expected, return immediately
  if (atomic_load(addr) != expected) return EAGAIN;

  // Slow path: enqueue and block
  futex_t f = {addr, get_wait_queue(addr), current->robust_list};
  add_to_robust_list(&f);
  smp_mb(); // Memory barrier before sleep
  return wait_event_interruptible(f.wq, atomic_load(addr) != expected);
}
```

## Test Matrix
| Race Condition | Expected Behavior |
|----------------|------------------|
| Thread exits during wait | Robust list cleanup wakes waiters |
| Wake before wait | Waiter sees updated value, returns immediately |
| Requeue race | Waiter moves to new queue without lost wakeups |

## Why This Is Asked
Interacting workers faulting the same page risks double work and stale data; userfaultfd paths require precise coordination to avoid data races and ensure memory visibility across cores. The scenario tests low-level synchronization, memory ordering, and fault handling.

## Key Concepts
- Per-page state machine (UNLOADED, LOADING, READY)
- Atomic CAS and release/acquire semantics
- Futex or condition variable waiting for other threads
- Correct interaction with userfaultfd and page fault semantics
- Performance trade-offs of fine-grained locking vs batching

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo C: per-page loading protocol
typedef enum { UNLOADED=0, LOADING=1, READY=2 } page_state_t;
typedef struct { _Atomic int state; /* 0/1/2 */ } page_t;

void access_page(page_t *p, void *dst) {
  int s = atomic_load_explicit(&p->state, memory_order_acquire);
  if (s == READY) { /* copy data to dst from page */ return; }
  if (s == UNLOADED) {
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&p->state, &UNLOADED, LOADING)) {
      // fault will be resolved by loader
      trigger_fault_and_load(p);
      atomic_store_explicit(&p->state, READY, memory_order_release);
      wake_waiters(p);
      /* copy data to dst */
      return;
    }
  }
  // wait until READY
  wait_on_futex(&p->state, READY);
  /* copy data */
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to NUMA and cache-line alignment?
- What failure modes exist if loader crashes or out-of-memory occurs?

